1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,472
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,472 --> 00:00:04,945
Episode 22.

3
00:00:04,945 --> 00:00:07,608
The Rainbow That Moved Away.

4
00:00:07,608 --> 00:00:10,502
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:10,502 --> 00:00:15,176
Why could the children not walk to a solid rainbow beside the tree?

6
00:00:15,176 --> 00:00:16,698
Ready?

7
00:00:16,698 --> 00:00:18,804
Let us find out.

8
00:00:18,804 --> 00:00:23,124
A rain shower ends, and sunlight appears behind the children.

9
00:00:23,125 --> 00:00:27,187
Ahead of them, a rainbow stretches across the sky.

10
00:00:27,187 --> 00:00:30,597
Its end is near that tree, Leo says.

11
00:00:30,597 --> 00:00:33,328
We could walk over and see it.

12
00:00:33,328 --> 00:00:36,480
They move along the path with Ms. Park.

13
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:39,687
The rainbow still seems farther away.

14
00:00:39,687 --> 00:00:42,527
It is escaping, says Pip.

15
00:00:42,527 --> 00:00:45,298
A very colorful suspect.

16
00:00:45,298 --> 00:00:50,624
Ms. Park explains that a rainbow is not a solid object resting on the ground.

17
00:00:50,624 --> 00:00:54,932
It is an effect of light reaching their eyes from water droplets.

18
00:00:54,932 --> 00:00:57,975
Sunlight contains different colors.

19
00:00:57,975 --> 00:01:02,160
As light enters and leaves droplets, it changes direction.

20
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:07,323
Reflection inside the drops also helps send some light back toward an observer.

21
00:01:07,323 --> 00:01:09,701
Maya turns around.

22
00:01:09,701 --> 00:01:13,817
She cannot see the same rainbow in the opposite direction.

23
00:01:13,817 --> 00:01:17,581
So where I stand and where I look matter, she says.

24
00:01:17,581 --> 00:01:19,456
Exactly.

25
00:01:19,456 --> 00:01:23,763
Your position, the sunlight, and the droplets all matter.

26
00:01:23,763 --> 00:01:26,399
Leo looks at the tree again.

27
00:01:26,399 --> 00:01:29,755
There is no colorful door underneath it.

28
00:01:29,755 --> 00:01:32,282
Pip offers to draw one.

29
00:01:32,282 --> 00:01:35,937
He can make a rainbow bridge that stays on paper.

30
00:01:35,937 --> 00:01:39,945
The club sits under a shelter and sketches what they saw.

31
00:01:39,945 --> 00:01:44,673
Maya includes the Sun behind the observer and raindrops ahead.

32
00:01:44,673 --> 00:01:49,103
Leo draws a little person asking a rainbow for directions.

33
00:01:49,103 --> 00:01:51,711
What does the rainbow answer?

34
00:01:51,711 --> 00:01:53,858
Maya asks.

35
00:01:53,858 --> 00:01:59,524
You can enjoy the view, Leo says, but you cannot use me as a shortcut to school.

36
00:01:59,524 --> 00:02:02,540
Now, a little more to discover.

37
00:02:02,540 --> 00:02:07,010
A rainbow forms when sunlight interacts with water droplets.

38
00:02:07,010 --> 00:02:13,451
Refraction bends the light, and reflection inside the droplets helps return it toward the observer.

39
00:02:13,451 --> 00:02:16,331
Different colors are separated.

40
00:02:16,331 --> 00:02:22,649
The rainbow you see depends on your viewpoint, so it is not a fixed object with an end you can reach.

41
00:02:22,649 --> 00:02:25,679
Let us hear the main idea again.

42
00:02:25,679 --> 00:02:30,407
Walking toward a rainbow did not bring the children to a colored object.

43
00:02:30,407 --> 00:02:35,665
The rainbow was produced by light, water droplets, and their viewing position.

44
00:02:35,665 --> 00:02:40,135
Drawing the arrangement helped them understand the beautiful effect.

45
00:02:40,135 --> 00:02:43,138
Here are three useful expressions.

46
00:02:43,138 --> 00:02:46,182
You can listen, or say them with me.

47
00:02:46,182 --> 00:02:48,709
It seems to be over there.

48
00:02:48,709 --> 00:02:51,236
It seems to be over there.

49
00:02:51,236 --> 00:02:53,668
Our position matters.

50
00:02:53,668 --> 00:02:56,100
Our position matters.

51
00:02:56,100 --> 00:02:59,211
Let us look from another point of view.

52
00:02:59,211 --> 00:03:02,323
Let us look from another point of view.

53
00:03:02,323 --> 00:03:05,190
Remember our listening question?

54
00:03:05,190 --> 00:03:09,864
Why could the children not walk to a solid rainbow beside the tree?

55
00:03:09,864 --> 00:03:12,051
Here is the answer.

56
00:03:12,051 --> 00:03:20,054
A rainbow is an optical effect that depends on light, droplets, and the observer's position, not a solid object on the ground.

57
00:03:20,054 --> 00:03:23,845
And here is a question just for your imagination.

58
00:03:23,845 --> 00:03:27,907
Where else have you seen light separate into different colors?

59
00:03:27,907 --> 00:03:31,073
There is more than one possible answer.

60
00:03:31,073 --> 00:03:34,225
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
